  ],
  "text": "The computer that landed on the Moon had less memory than a musical greeting card. A few tens of kilobytes. Your phone carries millions of times more. Its permanent memory was woven. Wires threaded through rings, by hand, in a factory. Through a ring meant one. Around the outside meant nought. Once woven it could never change. And during the descent it started raising alarms. Twelve oh two. It was overloaded. More work was arriving than it could possibly finish. But it had been written to rank every task, drop the lowest, and restart cleanly. So it threw away the radar work it did not need, and kept flying the landing. A computer that simply crashed there would have ended the mission. This one shed load and carried on. Its finest moment was not what it could do. It was knowing what to ignore. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
